  <section class="wrap fiche-body">
    <div>
      <p>Looking for a restaurant that accepts dogs in Port-Grimaud? Coco tested Le Grand Pin in person: dogs welcome on the terrace, right on the canals.</p>

      <h2>The vibe</h2>
      <p>In the heart of Port-Grimaud, almost with its feet in the water, Le Grand Pin serves traditional southern French cuisine on a covered terrace overlooking the port's canals — a pleasant spot to extend a stroll along the quays with your dog. The place mixes passing tourists with local regulars, a good sign of its consistency.</p>

      <h2>What to know before you go</h2>
      <p>Coco Explore ate here in person: very good food, a warm atmosphere, and two dogs settled at neighbouring terrace tables without any issue. According to an informal conversation on-site, dogs are welcome on the terrace, with no strict rule announced (size, muzzle) beyond ordinary common sense — it's best to simply mention it when you sit down. Two other practical points were reported to us the same way: the restaurant is said to be open roughly from early April to late October, and reportedly takes no reservations at all — you just show up, and if there's room, you eat.</p>
      <div class="callout">Coco's tip: use the meal to extend your walk along the canals of Port-Grimaud, one of the few villages built around its own marina.</div>

      <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <h3>Are dogs allowed at Le Grand Pin?</h3>
      <p>Yes, on the terrace — seen in person by Coco Explore.</p>
      <h3>Do you need to book?</h3>
      <p>No, according to what we were told on-site: first come, first served.</p>
      <h3>Is the restaurant open all year?</h3>
      <p>No, roughly from early April to late October, according to what we were told on-site.</p>

      <h2>Nearby</h2>
      <p>The medieval village of Grimaud and its dog-friendly restaurants (Le Café de France, La Baie des Voiles) are a few minutes away by car, up on the hillside.</p>
